    "faq": [
      {
        "q": "How accurate is BMI as an individual health metric?",
        "a": "BMI is an epidemiological screening metric, not an individual clinical diagnosis. While it reliably tracks statistical cardiometabolic risk across large population groups, it does not differentiate between skeletal muscle mass, bone density, and body fat distribution. For an individual, BMI provides the greatest clinical value when interpreted alongside waist circumference, body fat percentage, and routine blood panels."
      },
      {
        "q": "Why do Asian BMI thresholds differ from standard WHO ranges?",
        "a": "Research published in The Lancet (2004) and WHO expert consultations established that Asian populations often exhibit higher body fat percentages and elevated visceral adiposity at lower body weights. To enable earlier preventive screening, public health guidelines set lower risk action points for Asian adults: 23.0 kg/m² for increased risk (overweight) and 27.5 kg/m² for high risk (obesity), compared to standard cutoffs of 25.0 and 30.0 kg/m²."
      },
      {
        "q": "Can athletes and muscular individuals have a high BMI?",
        "a": "Yes. Skeletal muscle tissue has a significantly higher density (~1.06 g/cm³) than adipose tissue (~0.90 g/cm³). Strength athletes and bodybuilders frequently register BMI values above 25.0 or 30.0 despite maintaining low body fat levels (often below 12–15%). In these instances, BMI overestimates adiposity, and metrics like waist-to-height ratio or DEXA scans offer a far more accurate assessment."
      },
      {
        "q": "Why should you check waist-to-height ratio alongside BMI?",
        "a": "The waist-to-height ratio directly evaluates intra-abdominal visceral fat surrounding vital organs. Visceral fat is strongly linked to insulin resistance, type 2 diabetes, and hypertension. An established, evidence-based rule for adults across diverse backgrounds is to maintain your waist circumference below half your height (WHtR < 0.50)."
      },
      {
        "q": "What does a BMI score of 25.0 represent?",
        "a": "A BMI of 25.0 kg/m² marks the standard statistical threshold between healthy weight and overweight under WHO guidelines. It is not an immediate clinical diagnosis, but rather an educational screening benchmark indicating that lifestyle factors—including nutrition, physical activity, and blood pressure—should be reviewed with a qualified physician."
      },
      {
        "q": "What is a healthy BMI reference range for adult men and women?",
        "a": "Under WHO guidelines, a BMI of 18.5 to 24.9 is the healthy range. For Asian adults, the target range is 18.5 to 22.9. Women naturally carry more essential body fat than men. However, standard BMI ranges apply to both sexes."
      },
      {
        "q": "Why is visceral fat more hazardous than subcutaneous fat?",
        "a": "Visceral fat sits deep around inner organs. It is more active and can harm metabolic health. In contrast, fat under the skin stores normal energy. Your waist size reflects heart and health risks better than scale weight alone."
      },
      {
        "q": "How do aging and sarcopenia affect BMI interpretation in older adults?",
        "a": "After age 60, natural muscle loss can hide gains in body fat. A person can have a normal BMI while carrying less muscle. For older adults, a slightly higher BMI of 23.0 to 27.0 is often healthier. It helps protect against bone loss and weakness."
      },
      {
        "q": "How do BMR and TDEE formulas complement BMI screening?",
        "a": "BMI checks your height and weight. Metabolic formulas estimate the calories you burn each day. Basal Metabolic Rate (BMR) measures energy burned at rest. Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) adds your daily movement and exercise. This gives you clear targets to maintain or lose weight safely."
      },
